to start with a silly fun question how  long did it take you guys to learn and  actually get the title right  because i feel like sometimes i just  keep calling the show the woman in the  house across the street from the guy  that also lives there because it feels  like any variation kind of works but  did you have to practice to get that  right  i got it right right away and i that's  not a brag that's because it felt like a  wonderful joke and i think when someone  does a really good bit or makes a really  good joke that's just tonally perfect it  i tend to remember it i feel like this  title really lets the audience know that  we are we are going to be making fun of  things and this might not be as serious  as they think michael and tom how about  for you guys like when you explain this  show to your friends or family  do you get the title right  i mean  yeah and i don't know if it's it's  because i mean i guess i have practice  because i've said it more than some  people would say but i explaining that  title there's a real there's like a a  little joy to being able to say oh  that's what i'm shooting and that's  what's going to be on soon the woman in  the house across the street from the gun  on the window because you see people  think that the title should have ended  it should have ended five seconds ago  and you're still going and there's  there's a joy in that for sure  the title is definitely tied into the  experience right having lived through  this experience of making this project  you kind of think  that's it  i mean you could even probably add more  like  it is  it is such a such an appropriate title  for this experience that i think the  viewers will have  the title is too much and the show is  too much exactly and that's why it works  because titles are supposed to to be to  economize language  to let you know just a tiny little hint  of what you're about to get into and we  tell you almost everything and we tell  you mostly non-pertinent details about  the show in the title that's kind of why  the whole bit works  when you all learned who the actual  killer is and you read the script and  you saw how it all plays out  what was your reaction and and how do  you think viewers are going to react  i  could never in my wildest dreams and i  spent a lot of years playing a detective  on television um i  could never have guessed that this is  how they would have ended it but it  feels perfect  i i think the most shocking thing was  how violent the ending got  from my perspective  tom what about you i mean how did you  react to learning how things would end  up  i mean i i i love the end it feels  it feels perfect for what the show is  while at the same time being completely  unexpected and like like kristen said it  was incredibly violent and i was there  for the violence i didn't do anything  about it but it was it was um  there was some brutal stuff going down  michael was there anything that you were  particularly surprised about was it just  the ending that surprised you were there  moments along the way that surprised you  was it a lot of oh my god i can't  believe this is where they're taking  this yeah there's a lot of oh my god  this is where we're going like there it  was it was a roller coaster ride from  the moment i read  you know the scripts i mean i i was just  having these laugh out loud  moments in these big gasps every time i  read this you know the scripts and so  you know they're they just they didn't  feel like they had limits you know the  writers really felt like they they  didn't have any limit  to how absurd or how outrageous they  were going to make a situation to be and  it was it was a joy to actually see them  kind of push the boundaries especially  with this particular genre i was so  impressed with how this show takes on  everything with these types of stories  from the wine to the rain to the attic  all of the misdirection  adding in grief and a love story i mean  just everything is in this and somehow  it manages to all work  were you ever worried that you were  taking on too much and throwing too many  things into the mix or was everybody  always giving suggestions for things to  add i mean i certainly didn't give  suggestions to add anything because  these writers you know larry hugh and  rachel  um who i've known for a while they're  trained groundlings they're trained  writers they're they're very very good  at what they do and rachel has read and  watched every psychological thriller  ever and is particularly interested in  the ones that are written by women for  women and what that entails and so i  didn't add anything but i actually felt  like unless they went for broke and like  michael said were completely uninhibited  about what kind of absurdity they would  throw at you and unless they made fun or  commented on everything in this genre it  wouldn't work  this is a show that we had to we we  acted with subtlety but nothing about  the world of the show is subtle it's all  very in your face  kristen  you also got to do the final scenes with  the wild cameo that everybody will talk  about by the end of the show  what was it like to shoot that  especially with who you got to shoot it  with it felt like  uh the psychological thriller gods were  blessing us  because the the queen of all  psychological thrillers  um  having put  effort into even reading the email with  the invitation to be a part of this  was so thrilling and and  it almost allowed uh it made me feel  safe to poke fun at this genre knowing  that she gave it her blessing michael  what was it like to explore this  relationship but the relationship  between your characters is  serious it's dramatic it's sad i mean  what was it like to  really kind of have all of that drama in  the middle of all this craziness  you know i've had my fair share of of  love stories that i've that i've  participated in and there was none  there was never one that was like this i  mean this one just had so many more  layers to it it had so many more  um just uh  twists and turns and  you know trauma quite honestly like you  know this this relationship was rooted  in trauma and so in a lot of ways it was  uh much more complicated and as  christian was saying earlier the most  complicated part of it was the tone you  know being hopelessly in love but also  kind of  kind of an idiot about it you know what  i mean like love can make you an idiot  and that that's how i felt douglas was  in a lot of ways  tom your character is someone that we  learn about through the relationships  that he has with the women in his life  from his dead wife to his neighbor to  his girlfriend  his daughter  what was it like to explore  each of those  relationships and and find the character  through all these different sides to him  because he sort of feels like a  different person with each  person in his life  well i think that's partially because a  lot of what you're seeing is anna's  perspective of him and so he has to  she is constantly remaking in her mind  who the man is whether he is the great  hope that she's been waiting for her  life or whether he's a murderer and she  she bounces back and forth and some of  those relationships that you see you're  not seeing the truth you're saying  how um anna is imagining it so yeah it's  very much about playing the role that is  simultaneously the man he is the man i  know he is but it's all revealed in  episode four and  at the same time trying to play the guy  that's anna's invention  kristen i i absolutely love the  character of people because i i also  love cameron britain  it's hilarious and insane that he pretty  much exists just to fix a mailbox which  kind of makes him the worst handyman  ever  so how much fun was it to have that  dynamic as really sort of the only  constant that runs throughout this whole  show  it was  a lot of fun because not only was it the  constant but it was the usually when  there's a constant you know when there's  a wise old tree in a cartoon you go back  to the constant you get new information  buell's character gives absolutely no  information  or is not helpful in any way he's just  the constant and so anna doesn't really  know where to place him other than like  he's just always there and then as the  story unravels and you you know there's  this wonderful line that i have to  michael's character when i say um  what was buell like when you hired him  to fix our mailbox all those years ago  and just that line it kind of sums up  what we're doing like oh and you go oh  my god he's been fixing that mailbox for  years and if you really watch cameron's  performance he's so great because he's  just he's picking things up and he's  looking at them and he's that's my to-do  list he's picking things up and he's  like just a screwdriver he analyzes  everything before he touches it it's  great and it's absolutely pointless  which is perfect  yeah i loved everything about it i loved  this show i had such a blast watching it  and  really kudos to all of you for pulling  this off because it's it shouldn't have  been able to be and it is so thank you  thank you
 

